# To-Do List Application (Streamlit)

An interactive to-do list built with Python. Tasks are represented as
objects (a `Task` class), not bare dictionaries — matches the "classes"
option in your task's skill list rather than the "dictionaries" option.

## Features

- Add tasks (rejects empty input and duplicate active tasks)
- Mark tasks complete/incomplete via checkbox
- Delete individual tasks
- Filter view: All / Active / Completed
- Bulk actions: clear all completed, delete everything
- Live counters for total/active/completed tasks
- Each task stores a description, completion status, creation timestamp,
  and a unique ID

## Requirements

- Python 3.8+
- `streamlit`

## Setup

```bash
pip install streamlit
```

## Run

```bash
streamlit run todo_list.py
```

Opens in your browser at `http://localhost:8501`.

## Why a class instead of a dictionary

Your task lists "Dictionaries or Classes" as an either/or. I used a
`@dataclass`:

```python
@dataclass
class Task:
    description: str
    completed: bool = False
    created_at: str = ...
    id: str = ...
```

This is a deliberate choice, not the only correct one. A dataclass gives
you attribute access (`task.completed`) instead of key access
(`task["completed"]`), and each task gets a stable unique `id` so it can
be found and deleted correctly even if two tasks have the identical
description. If your assignment specifically wants dictionaries instead
(some rubrics check for that literally), it's a straightforward rewrite —
say so and I'll convert it.

## Known limitations

- **No persistence.** Tasks live in `st.session_state`, which is wiped
  when you restart the app or close the browser tab. There's no file or
  database backing this, so nothing survives a restart. If your rubric
  expects tasks to persist between sessions, that needs actual storage
  (e.g. a JSON file or SQLite) — different task, tell me if you want that
  added.
- No editing an existing task's text — you can only complete or delete
  it, not rename it. Add if the rubric requires it.
- No due dates, priorities, or categories — out of scope for the stated
  task, but easy to bolt on if asked.

